\section{Introduction}

Biological ontologies such as NCBITaxon and the Relations Ontology (RO) 
encode hierarchical, relational, and evolutionary information across 
millions of entities. Traditional ontology systems are static and 
non-executable, limiting their use in dynamic ecological or evolutionary 
simulation. OOBSE-v1.0 addresses this gap by converting semantic triples 
into sparse tensor graphs, embedding taxonomic structures into hyperbolic 
manifolds, and evolving ecological state variables through symplectic 
Hamiltonian dynamics.

The engine is designed for secure, large-scale biological simulation 
under post-quantum cryptographic constraints. This work presents the 
architecture, mathematical foundations, cryptographic envelope, 
simulation kernel, and empirical validation of OOBSE-v1.0.

\section{Ontology Ingestion and Tensor Construction}

Biological entities $e_i \in \mathcal{E}$ and relations $r_k \in \mathcal{R}$ 
are represented as a directed acyclic graph:

\[
\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E}_{\mathrm{edges}}, \mathbf{W})
\]

Each semantic triple $(S,P,O)$ is mapped into a sparse relational tensor:

\[
\mathbf{\mathcal{X}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N \times K}
\]

